// expect-exit: 0
//
// PROBE 07 — `string_bytes` at the borrow edges.
//
// ASKS: `string_bytes(s)` into a scanner, the slice's `.len` and `.ptr`, and
// which of D-004's four escape edges the compiler ACTUALLY refuses -- which
// `0.0.0.md` §4 is careful to say "is not the same question as which the
// specification says it does".
//
// DEPENDS ON THE ANSWER: RX-054 makes `uint8[]` the primitive and every entry
// point in `API.md` takes one.
//
// ==========================================================================
// THREE OF THE FOUR EDGES WERE ALREADY ANSWERED. READ THIS BEFORE EXTENDING.
// ==========================================================================
//
// Edges 2 and 3 -- a view RETURNED, and a view STORED IN A STRUCT that outlives
// the call -- are **O-N9**, a CONFIRMED compiler defect, independently verified,
// accepted as the compiler's **DEF-3**, and scheduled as the second commit of
// its cycle 1.5.1b. Both are ACCEPTED where D-004 rule 2 says they must be
// refused, and reading the result afterwards reads freed memory: the six-case
// contrast set at `../nitpick-time/tests/probe/defect/view_escape/` returns
// **170** from case 5, which is `0xAA`, the runtime's free-poison (D-183).
//
// **This probe does not re-run them and does not raise them again.** Nothing
// below is built on the escape rule being enforced, and `probe06b` records this
// library's one genuine addition to that report -- the SAFE slice return, of a
// subrange of a parameter, that a naive fix would also refuse.
//
// WHAT THIS PROBE ADDS is edge 4, which the six cases do not cover, and the
// answer is not the one the shape of O-N9 predicts. See §4 below.
//
// THE HOUSE RULE, STATED AT THE RIGHT STRENGTH. `nitpick-time`'s formulation is
// "a view is a parameter, never a return value", and it was written
// deliberately conservatively because at the time there was no way to tell the
// safe cases from the dangerous ones. **There is now**, because DEF-3's checker
// is being written and it draws the line in a particular place:
//
//   * a view of a **frame-local owner** must not escape — that is the bug;
//   * a view whose root is a **pointer-shaped binding** (a wild pointer, a
//     slice, a `cstring`) is the POINTEE'S borrow and may travel, so
//     `string_from_bytes(buf, n)` over an alloc'd block, returned, stays legal;
//   * a view of a **TEMPORARY** is refused outright, `NITPICK-BORROW-012`:
//     `string_bytes(string_concat(a, b))` returned must bind the intermediate
//     first. That composes with D-246 — the `string_concat` is an owning
//     temporary that leaks today as well — so the shape is doubly wrong and
//     both faults have the same fix: bind it.
//
// So this file does NOT conclude "a view must never be returned". That is too
// strong and would be wrong the moment DEF-3 lands. What §1's functions obey is
// the narrower and permanent rule: **they return VALUES, and they take views as
// parameters**, which is right under either regime and is the shape `src/` will
// actually have.
mod:probe07_string_bytes_edges;

error:E9;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1 — EDGE 1: PASSED DOWN. Legal under D-004 and always was: the callee's
// frame is strictly inner, so the viewed storage outlives the call. This is the
// shape of every entry point the library will ever have.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A scanner step: takes the view, returns a NUMBER and never the view.
func:count_byte = int64(uint8[]:src, uint8:want) never fails {
    int64:i = 0i64;
    int64:n = 0i64;
    while (i < src.len) {
        if (src[i] == want) { n = n + 1i64; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    pass n;
};

// Two frames deep, because "passed down" must survive being passed down again --
// this is `regex_find` calling an engine calling a byte-class test.
func:count_two_deep = int64(uint8[]:src, uint8:a, uint8:b) never fails {
    pass (raw count_byte(src, a)) + (raw count_byte(src, b));
};

// A view NARROWED and passed down. The narrowing is a `...` range on the way
// IN, which is legal for the same reason: it never travels up.
func:count_in_span = int64(uint8[]:src, int64:lo, int64:hi, uint8:want) never fails {
    pass (raw count_byte(src[lo...hi], want));
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2 — the async machinery for edge 4. `step` exists only to create a genuine
// suspension point between the taking of a view and the reading of it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

async func:step = int64(int64:v) {
    pass v + 1i64;
};

// EDGE 4: a `uint8[]` HELD ACROSS AN `await`. The view is a parameter, the
// suspension happens between binding it and reading it, and `src.len` is read
// on the far side.
async func:view_across_await = int64(uint8[]:src) {
    int64:n = relay await step(0i64);
    pass src.len + n;
};

// THE CONTROL, and it is the line that decides how this finding is written up:
// the SAME shape with an `@`-borrow in place of the slice. In O-N9 the control
// is refused and the slice is accepted, and that asymmetry is the defect. Here
// they behave identically -- see §4.
async func:borrow_across_await = int64(int64->:p) {
    int64:n = relay await step(0i64);
    pass (<-p) + n;
};

async func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    // "abcaabXbb", nine bytes. `s` is owned by this frame throughout.
    string:s = string_concat("abcaa", "bXbb");
    uint8[]:v = string_bytes(s);

    // ---- §3 — the slice's own members ---------------------------------

    // `.len` is the byte length -- the same number `string_byte_length` gives,
    // because `string_bytes` is a VIEW and not a copy (D-185: same pointer,
    // same length, no allocation).
    if (v.len != 9i64)                                  { exit 10i32; }
    if (string_byte_length(s) != 9i64)                  { exit 11i32; }

    // `.ptr` exists and is readable. The library never dereferences it -- every
    // read goes through the bounds-checked index (D-070, `SAFETY.md` §5) -- but
    // it is what `string_from_bytes(ptr, len)` takes, so it is pinned here.
    discard(v.ptr);

    // An empty view is legal and has length zero, which is the case every
    // engine hits on an empty haystack.
    uint8[]:empty = v[0i64...0i64];
    if (empty.len != 0i64)                              { exit 12i32; }

    // ---- §1 in practice — edge 1 -----------------------------------------

    if ((raw count_byte(v, 97u8)) != 3i64)              { exit 20i32; }   // 'a'
    if ((raw count_byte(v, 98u8)) != 4i64)              { exit 21i32; }   // 'b'
    if ((raw count_byte(v, 122u8)) != 0i64)             { exit 22i32; }   // 'z'
    if ((raw count_two_deep(v, 97u8, 98u8)) != 7i64)    { exit 23i32; }
    // The narrowed span "aab" (indices 3..6) holds two 'a's.
    if ((raw count_in_span(v, 3i64, 6i64, 97u8)) != 2i64) { exit 24i32; }
    if ((raw count_in_span(v, 0i64, 9i64, 98u8)) != 4i64) { exit 25i32; }

    // The view still reads correctly after all of that: nothing a callee did
    // disturbed the caller's storage.
    if (v[0i64] != 97u8)                                { exit 26i32; }
    if (v[8i64] != 98u8)                                { exit 27i32; }

    // ---- §4 — EDGE 4, and the control ------------------------------------

    // Both of these compile and both run. See the header block below §4 for
    // what that does and does not establish.
    int64:r = await view_across_await(v) ?! E9;
    if (r != 10i64)                                     { exit 30i32; }

    int64:x = 4i64;
    int64:rb = await borrow_across_await(@x) ?! E9;
    if (rb != 5i64)                                     { exit 31i32; }

    // D-151 counts `wild` blocks, D-188 counts live drivers, and neither sees a
    // managed body. This program takes no `wild` bytes and spawns no driver, so
    // it cannot trip either on any exit path; `s`'s body is managed and dies
    // with its binding. Exit 0 is therefore silent about leaks here rather than
    // a proof of their absence.
    exit 0i32;
};

// ===========================================================================
// §4 — WHAT EDGE 4 MEASURED, AND WHY IT IS NOT REPORTED AS A DEFECT
// ===========================================================================
//
// MEASURED on compiler 950bb1d: BOTH `view_across_await` and
// `borrow_across_await` compile with no diagnostic and run to exit 0.
//
// `TYPE_REFERENCE.md` §9.2.1 says a slice "cannot cross a thread spawn or an
// `await`", so on the face of it that is edge 4 unenforced. **It is not written
// up as an O-N9 sibling, and the control is the reason.** O-N9 is an ASYMMETRY:
// the `@`-borrow is refused `NITPICK-BORROW-001` and the slice is not, and the
// two spellings sitting side by side are what make it under-enforcement rather
// than a design question. Here the two spellings behave the SAME, so there is no
// asymmetry to point at, and "the compiler does not check this" is a claim about
// an unimplemented rung rather than a bug -- a distinction W-11 turns on.
//
// AND THE CLAIM THIS PROBE CAN HONESTLY MAKE IS NARROWER THAN THE RULE. What is
// tested above is a view held across a suspension in a callee **that its caller
// awaits directly**, with the viewed storage in the caller's frame and alive
// throughout. Nothing here dies. D-177 makes crossing locals frame-resident and
// the async frame is heap-allocated, so this shape may well be sound. The shape
// the rule is really aimed at is a borrow crossing a `spawn` -- handed to a task
// that outlives the frame it was taken in -- and THIS PROBE DOES NOT TEST THAT.
//
// IT IS NOT TESTED HERE ON PURPOSE. `nregex` is synchronous by construction:
// RX-008 forbids syscalls, RX-061 says matching cannot fail, trap or allocate,
// and no function in `API.md` is `async`. An edge this library cannot reach is
// not worth a probe that would have to stand up a task to answer. Recorded as a
// bounded measurement, and left for a library that actually spawns.

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (E9)               { exit 80i32; },
        (DeadlineExceeded) { exit 88i32; },
        (DriverLeak)       { exit 89i32; },
        (HeapBadRequest)   { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)          { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)      { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)      { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)      { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)         { exit 96i32; },
        (*)                { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
